# Basic inland letter rate, end-year, in old pence. 1900 = 100 by construction.
# Before 1840 the rate varied with distance and the number of sheets; the series
# uses the single-letter rate for 100 miles, which is close to the measured 1837
# average of 9.5d per letter: 4d from 1711, 5d from 1784, 7d from 1797, 8d from
# 1805, 9d from 1812. The Uniform Fourpenny Post of Dec 1839 lasted five weeks and
# is not given a year of its own; the penny rate applies from 1840.
# After 1840: 1d to 1915, 1.5d from 1918, 2d in 1920, back to 1.5d in 1922,
# 2.5d from 1940, 3d from 1957, 4d from 1965; from 1968 the ordinary service is
# the 2nd class rate. 2050 holds real postage level, rising with the general index.
# Priority pays 250% of this rate (class_revenue_percent[1] in goods-128.dat).
# The premium it represents has been: the old distance tariff against the penny
# post, 1840-1914 (about 9.5x); Empire air mail at the ordinary rate, 1915-38;
# the wartime air surcharge (6x); post-war air mail (about 2x); and first against
# second class (1.2-2.0x) since 1968. The multiplier is fixed, so 250% is the
# equal-weight geometric mean of those premiums (2.49), deliberately rich for the
# modern era. Normal is the class that is excluded by price, so the player decides
# when to open the cheap service.
mail rate=1750,400,1784,500,1797,700,1805,800,1812,900,1840,100,1918,150,1920,200,1922,150,1940,250,1957,300,1965,400,1971,600,1973,720,1974,840,1975,1560,1977,1680,1979,1920,1980,2400,1981,2760,1982,3000,1984,3120,1985,2880,1986,3120,1988,3360,1989,3600,1990,4080,1991,4320,1993,4560,1996,4800,1999,4560,2003,4800,2004,5040,2006,5520,2007,5760,2008,6480,2009,7200,2010,7680,2011,8640,2012,12000,2014,12720,2015,12960,2016,13200,2017,13440,2018,13920,2019,14640,2020,15600,2021,15840,2022,16320,2023,18000,2024,20400,2025,20880,2026,21840,2050,36519
# Goods. 1900 = 100. Average nominal goods revenue per ton-mile (GB); scales the per-good
# staged rates in goods-128.dat. Anchor: the pre-war charged average of 1.235d per ton-mile
# = 100, derived as the measured January-August 1920 blend (1.413d, before the 1920 increases)
# divided by the documented wartime increases of 1913 (+4%) and 1917 (+10%); the derivation
# self-validates at the join. The .dat base rates are the c. 1890s statutory maximum schedule
# (Ackworth p. 145, converted), so the index carries relative change only.
# 1750-1830: canal and waterway charged rates (Manchester-Liverpool 6s/ton c. 1760; midlands
# canals 4.06d/ton-mile 1795), falling to 100 across 1825-1845 as railway competition takes
# the traffic. 1845-1912 flat: statutory rates fixed by the authorising Acts; the 1888-94
# revision left charged rates "absolutely unaffected" (Acworth p. 148). 1913 and 1917 the
# first general increases (+4%, +10%). Measured blends 1920-1931 from the monthly Ministry
# of Transport statistics (1920 is January-November; December receipts were not published).
# 1939-1941 on the documented charge steps (no ton-mile data was published). Measured BR
# figures 1938-1977 (Annual Abstract of Statistics; GERS RH087, RH062, RH071). 1975/1982
# from Serpell 1982 (new pence per net ton-mile; 1975 deflated with the general index).
# 2015-2024: ORR nominal freight income divided by Table 1310 tonne-km (financial years
# labelled by starting year). Gaps (1932-1937, 1966, 1968-1971, 1973-1975, 1983-2014) are
# left to interpolation. 2025-2050 holds the 2024 real level and tracks the general index.
# Evidence: ai/goods-rate-evidence.md. Series design: ai/prices-goods-rate.md.
