# Vehicles. 1900 = 100. Nominal purchase price of transport equipment, chained from three
# sources, each linked on an overlap:
#   1856-1938  Feinstein, National Income, Expenditure and Output of the United Kingdom
#              1855-1965 (1972), table 63 column 6, railway rolling stock, 1913 = 100. The
#              1856-1920 part is an index of the cost of labour and materials, not of quoted
#              prices, so it measures a fixed specification and not a changing machine.
#   1938-1965  Feinstein table 63 column 4, plant and machinery, 1913 = 100, linked to the
#              rolling stock column at 1938 by a factor of 0.9432. This is not a vehicle
#              series. It is used because Feinstein's vehicle columns are blank for 1939-1947
#              and it is the only continuous published engineering-capital index that bridges
#              the war.
#   1965-1997  Blue Book 1997, tables 13.2 and 13.4, gross fixed capital formation, vehicles,
#              ships and aircraft, current prices divided by constant 1990 prices. The pair
#              returns exactly 1.0000 in 1990, and over its 1987-1997 overlap it tracks the
#              general index to within 0.3 per cent. The deflator is scaled so that its 1965
#              value equals 526.6 on Feinstein's 1913 = 100 basis.
# 1998-2050 holds the 1997 real level and tracks the general index. Two alternative tails were
# tested and rejected: the Blue Book 2026 pair DLWZ/DLWJ implies that vehicle prices fell 4 per
# cent between 1990 and 1997 while the general index rose 25 per cent, and the CPI series D7CO
# covers consumer cars only, with no railway rolling stock, ships, aircraft or commercial goods
# vehicles. Neither can carry the series, so the real level is held instead.
# Evidence: ai/vehicle-cost-evidence.md. Method: ai/vehicle-cost-method.md.
# Last updated: 2026-11-17.
