# Vehicle maintenance stays on the general index. No UK maintenance or repair price index
# exists before January 1987, and the series available from that date (RPI DOCT, CPI D7ED)
# measure private motor cars, not locomotives, carriages, wagons or ships. A series that is the
# general index for 237 years and motor cars for 39 years is worse than the fallback, which is
# at least stated. Revisit if a rail or engineering repair price series is found.
#vehicle maintenance=1750,100,2022,100
# Buildings. 1900 = 100. Nominal price of building work, chained from four sources, each
# linked on an overlap year:
#   1845-1938  Mitchell, British Historical Statistics (1988), Ch VII Table 8, "index of
#              building costs, 1930 = 100", reproduced in the Bank of England "A millennium
#              of macroeconomic data" sheet A32 column S.
#   1939-1954  Holmans, Historical Statistics of Housing in Britain, Table M.10, "index of
#              construction costs, 1958-60 = 100" (same sheet, column T). 1940-1947 are
#              absent from that sheet and come from Mitchell and Deane, Abstract of British
#              Historical Statistics (1962), Ch IX Table 4.
#   1955-2013  BIS Output Price Index, All New Construction, 2005 = 100 (BIS quarterly price
#              and cost indices, 2014 Q1 edition, Table 1, column 15); 2013 is the mean of
#              the four published quarters.
#   2014-2026  ONS Construction Output Price Index, all new work, 2015 = 100, annual means
#              of the monthly series (ONS dataset "Construction output price indices",
#              release of 2026-11-12). 2026 is the mean of January to September.
# Links: 1938-39 by continuity, following the join made in the Bank of England sheet; 1955
# on the overlap of the Holmans and BIS series; 2014 Q1 on the overlap of the BIS and ONS
# series. Before 1845 the 1845 real level is held and tracks general: a wage-based backcast
# fits the 1845-1914 Mitchell index worse (see prices-config.md). After 2026 the 2026 real
# level is held and tracks general to 2050.
# The engine applies this key to building purchase and maintenance cost, and to the depot,
# headquarters, found city, found industry, building removal, field removal and transformer
# costs and the transformer maintenance. The station, dock, roadstop, air terminal and post
# office multipliers follow the infrastructure key instead, as does a halt building priced
# from its level rather than from an explicit cost. New-build output prices are used
# throughout; repair and maintenance prices are not spliced in, so the series is a
# compromise for maintenance, which is more labour-intensive.
# Last updated: 2026-11-15.
